// A value object referenced ACROSS a context boundary — `Billing.Receipt.shipTo`
// resolves to `valueobject Addr` declared in the sibling context `Directory`,
// with nothing imported and nothing re-declared.
//
// Minted by the fixture-shape audit (docs/audits/2026-09-29-fixture-shape-coverage.md).
// NO model anywhere in this repo had this shape — not one of 431 fixtures,
// examples, journey stages or audit models — even though a type shared between
// two contexts is the ordinary DDD move and the README's own Quick Example
// shape.  `BoundedContextIR.siblingValueObjects` exists precisely to serve it,
// so every emitter that MATERIALISES a referenced VO has two lookup paths to
// keep in step: `ctx.valueObjects` for a local declaration and the sibling pool
// for a foreign one.  Nothing exercised the second.
//
// What it pins: the flattening must produce the SAME leaf columns whichever pool
// the VO came from.  `Directory.Person.home` and `Billing.Receipt.shipTo` are
// the same `Addr`, so `home_line1`/`home_geo_lat`/`home_geo_lng` and
// `ship_to_line1`/`ship_to_geo_lat`/`ship_to_geo_lng` must be structurally
// identical — same recursion, same leaf types, same NULLability.  `Addr.geo: Geo`
// keeps the nesting in play, because a cross-context lookup that succeeds at the
// first level and fails at the second is the likelier bug.
//
// The evaluation's F-008 is what the blindness cost: the consuming context
// emitted ONE column under the unflattened name while the owning context
// flattened correctly, so the migration and the ORM disagreed on column name AND
// type and only a live database could tell.  Per-backend state at mint time:
//
//   node    correct — `shipTo_line1`/`shipTo_geo_lat`/`shipTo_geo_lng` in the
//           drizzle schema, the migration DDL and both rehydrate paths.
//   dotnet  correct — `ReceiptConfiguration` nests `OwnsOne` and names
//           `ship_to_line1` / `ship_to_geo_lat` / `ship_to_geo_lng`.
//   java    correct — `@AttributeOverride(name = "line1", column = @Column(name
//           = "ship_to_line1"))`, nested path overridden the same way.
//   elixir  BROKEN TOO, by the SAME root cause as python.
//           `receipt_controller.ex` calls `serialize_addr(record.ship_to)` and
//           defines neither `serialize_addr/1` nor the nested `serialize_geo/1`,
//           while the OWNING context's `person_controller.ex` defines both —
//           `** (CompileError) undefined function serialize_addr/1`, so
//           `mix compile --warnings-as-errors` fails outright.  Excluded with
//           python in the manifest's `SIBLING_VO_RESOLUTION` set.
//
//           This entry first claimed elixir was free, on the reasoning that a VO
//           is one `:map` (jsonb) cell there so there is no flattening to get
//           wrong.  The reasoning was sound and the conclusion WRONG: flattening
//           is not the only artifact a VO needs, and the claim came from
//           INSPECTING the emitted schema instead of compiling the project.  CI's
//           `mix compile` found it in minutes.  Do not grade a backend here by
//           reading its output.
//   python  BROKEN, and this fixture is why we know.  `db/schema.py` and the
//           migration both create the three flattened columns, while
//           `receipt_repository.py` binds `"ship_to": aggregate.ship_to` on
//           insert, `root["ship_to"]` on upsert, and reads `ship_to=row.ship_to`
//           on hydrate — a column in NEITHER artifact.  Only the hydrate site is
//           a type error (`"ReceiptRow" has no attribute "ship_to"`); the two
//           bind sites are untyped dict literals, so `mypy --strict` catches 1
//           of the 3 and a live request fails on all 3.  The repository builder
//           resolves a VO through `ctx.valueObjects` only and falls through to
//           the scalar path for a sibling-context one.  python is excluded from
//           this row's `backends:` via the manifest's named
//           `SIBLING_VO_RESOLUTION` set, which carries this reading — an
//           exclusion, not a compile-skip, because a cell that only GENERATES is
//           refused by `gate-ledger.test.ts`.  The key returns when the
//           resolution lands.
//
// COMPILE-TIER ONLY — no `test e2e` block, for the same reason
// `find-bypass.ddd` and `projection-agg-filters.ddd` have none.  A recorded
// behavioural case must ship a reviewed wire golden
// (`test/behavioral/wire-golden/`, gated by `golden-coverage.test.ts`), and a
// golden can only be captured from a booted stack on every backend — which is
// impossible while python's and elixir's halves of this shape are broken (above): the
// recording would either fail or enshrine the defect as the answer key.  What
// this fixture proves is static and per-backend: the flattened column set the
// migration creates must equal the one the ORM/repository names, in BOTH
// contexts.  Add the runtime block, and capture its golden, in the same PR that
// fixes python.
system VoCrossContext {
  subdomain Commerce {
    context Directory {
      valueobject Geo {
        lat: decimal
        lng: decimal
      }

      valueobject Addr {
        line1: string
        geo: Geo
      }

      aggregate Person with crudish {
        name: string
        home: Addr
        derived display: string = name
      }

      repository Persons for Person { }
    }

    // The consuming context.  `Addr` is NOT declared here.
    context Billing {
      aggregate Receipt with crudish {
        reference: string
        shipTo: Addr
        derived display: string = reference
      }

      repository Receipts for Receipt { }
    }
  }

  api CommerceApi from Commerce
  storage primary { type: postgres }
  resource directoryState { for: Directory, kind: state, use: primary }
  resource billingState { for: Billing, kind: state, use: primary }

  deployable d {
    platform: __PLATFORM__
    contexts: [Directory, Billing]
    dataSources: [directoryState, billingState]
    serves: CommerceApi
    port: 4000
  }
}
